WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:10.680
If the world was ending, and you could save only&nbsp;
one hundred and sixteen images as the final record&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:18.280
of planet Earth, what kind of moments would&nbsp;
you preserve? This is an actual question NASA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:25.800
faced in 1977, when they decided to attach to the&nbsp;
Voyager spacecrafts golden records encoded with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:33.720
selection of photos intended to immortalize&nbsp;
life on our fragile planet. And when I look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:39.640
at these pictures, which have now left our solar&nbsp;
system and are hurtling out into the cosmic dark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:46.360
I can’t get out of my head how they will&nbsp;
probably, one day, be all that’s left of us. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:52.320
the vacuum of space, these images could last&nbsp;
for billions of years, outliving the earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:59.903
outliving the sun. And though that should&nbsp;
maybe be comforting… I think it scares me.&nbsp;

00:00:59.903 --> 00:01:10.520
(Title: Why Deep Space Scares Us)
(Space Chatter)&nbsp;

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:16.800
Space has long been a birthplace of existential&nbsp;
dread. Early astronaut programs were haunted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:23.360
by concerns of “Space Madness,” the theory that&nbsp;
humans would be mentally and spiritually broken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:30.200
by entering the celestial abyss. It makes sense.&nbsp;
Space is a deathtrap. Without a spacesuit the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:34.840
very blood in your veins would form bubbles&nbsp;
that block circulation, the neurons in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:40.920
brain exploding with pain until lack of oxygen&nbsp;
causes you to pass out — with death arriving just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:47.280
ninety seconds later. Even a successful voyage&nbsp;
involves a prolonged period in cramped seclusion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:53.960
surrounded by the hostile dark. Though “Space&nbsp;
Madness” is no longer an official term, anxiety,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:59.600
mood swings, and severe emotional distress have&nbsp;
all been documented in those who have touched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:08.040
the void. The idea that a human mind plunged&nbsp;
into the infinite sea of space might, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:15.040
drown, has crept into the foundations of how&nbsp;
we depict the cosmos. In 2001: A Space Odyssey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:20.720
the narrative begins in the shallows — depicting&nbsp;
a clean, orderly future of space travel, one that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:25.960
would have been comforting to its late 60’s&nbsp;
audience. Yet in both the film and the novel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:32.560
these early space scenes are a façade, a veneer&nbsp;
of fathomability concealing the gaping maw of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:38.120
the unfathomable. The deeper you descend into&nbsp;
the story, the clearer it becomes that all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:44.320
artificial gravity and neatly divided meals amount&nbsp;
to a futile attempt to give order to a chaotic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:51.120
universe. Like space itself, 2001 ultimately&nbsp;
proves immense and impenetrable, feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:58.800
like something the mind wasn’t meant to fully&nbsp;
encompass. A counterpoint to this vastness exists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:05.280
in Andrei Tarkovsky’s film Solaris. The story of&nbsp;
a psychologist sent to a research station where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:11.080
all crewmembers have become trapped within their&nbsp;
own memories, Solaris is technically another tale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:18.360
of space-born madness. Yet while 2001 spans from&nbsp;
the dawn of humanity to a nebulous future stage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:25.680
of evolution, the scale of Solaris is intensely&nbsp;
personal. Despite primarily taking place in space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:32.480
the film, like the characters, constantly flashes&nbsp;
back to small, intimate moments on Earth. Far from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:38.680
a story of humanity’s ascension, Solaris makes the&nbsp;
case that the maddening power of space has less to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:45.040
do with the stars, and more to do with trauma&nbsp;
we carry with us into the dark. Like preserved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:55.320
photographs, the past lingers — both a steadying&nbsp;
anchor and crushing weight. (Character Gasps) In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.320 --> 00:04:02.120
the face of a vast, indifferent universe, how&nbsp;
do we decide what moments to hold onto? The game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:09.480
Outer Wilds forces you to confront this question&nbsp;
— over, and over, and over again. You play as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:14.400
alien exploring the cosmos in a worryingly&nbsp;
low-tech spaceship, and the main thing you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:22.400
do in Outer Wilds is, well, die. The game is a&nbsp;
crash course in why space is so nightmarish. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:28.560
every failure is an opportunity to learn from your&nbsp;
mistakes, because — spoiler alert for the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:33.880
twenty-two minutes of the game: every time you&nbsp;
perish, you’re sent backwards to the start of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:41.560
time loop. It’s a mechanic that changes the value&nbsp;
of each moment. Though I remained cautious for my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:48.160
first few loops, I soon began to throw myself into&nbsp;
the void without care, all my fears of space blown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:55.600
away. Simultaneously, I began to skip over smaller&nbsp;
moments. Why waste time roasting a marshmallow or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:02.080
speaking with a friend if all this is going to&nbsp;
be reset anyways? …Yet at the end of each life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:08.560
Outer Wilds displays images depicting all you&nbsp;
accomplished, your very own Golden Record. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:14.760
a feature that subtly reframes what experiences&nbsp;
are significant. Watching the sunrise might not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:20.800
get you closer to beating the game, but when&nbsp;
your life literally flashes before your eyes…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:29.320
it’s the moment that stands out. Yet there’s this…&nbsp;
other moment that stands out. Late into the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:35.040
I became separated from my ship. I wasn’t too&nbsp;
nervous, at first. I knew I could definitely catch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:41.880
up with — oh wow, that’s really moving. Caught in&nbsp;
the gravity of a planet, my ship faded further and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:49.320
further out of view. Soon, it was hopeless… and as&nbsp;
I looked out across oblivion, I realized this loop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:55.720
wasn’t going to end until I ran out of oxygen. And&nbsp;
though I knew I’d wake up back where I’d started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.720 --> 00:06:03.600
the thought of drifting until then, waiting for my&nbsp;
inevitable demise, made me feel… Well, it reminded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:10.440
me of a historical fragment I’d learned long ago.&nbsp;
The first living thing to orbit the Earth was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:17.040
dog named Laika. Part of a Soviet experiment to&nbsp;
discover the harmful effects of space on a body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:23.640
Laika’s journey through the stars was designed to&nbsp;
be strictly one way, sent to space onboard a tiny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:29.520
metal coffin. It was assumed she’d die in&nbsp;
space from running out of oxygen — instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:36.400
the craft overheated. The last words she likely&nbsp;
ever heard was a biologist saying 'please forgive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:44.080
us.’ …My ill-fated drift through the cosmos didn’t&nbsp;
end when I ran out of oxygen either. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:50.240
my body slowly fell into the sun, the voyage&nbsp;
concluding in premature flames. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:55.360
unlike Laika, my journey then continued —&nbsp;
although the unsettling experience stayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.360 --> 00:07:01.760
with me. …Onboard her doomed craft, Laika’s&nbsp;
heartrate accelerated to three times its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:09.960
usual rate. She was terrified. Did she also feel&nbsp;
betrayed, I wonder? Did she understand enough of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:15.360
what was happening to feel betrayed? There’s&nbsp;
a photo of a dog on the golden record, meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:26.440
to commemorate the loyalty of our canine friends…&nbsp;
'Please forgive us,’ Laika. Should she forgive us?&nbsp;

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:32.880
‘The trajectory of this ship is unchanging.’
In Douglas Trumbull’s Silent Running, a vessel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:40.120
is on an unalterable path towards annihilation.&nbsp;
In Claire Denis’s High Life, a vessel is on an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:46.720
unalterable path towards annihilation. In Cecile&nbsp;
Richard’s Under a Star Called Sun, a vessel is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:53.480
on a… you get it. As a piece of interactive&nbsp;
fiction, Under a Star Called Sun stands out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:58.840
from the two films, telling through pixel art&nbsp;
and short snippets of text the story of a lone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:05.120
cosmonaut whose ship is drifting into the sun.&nbsp;
Yet despite their hopeless status, the explorer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:10.760
continues to care for the plants aboard their&nbsp;
condemned vessel. It’s a ritual the protagonists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:16.440
of all three pieces of media take part in for&nbsp;
different reasons. In Silent Running, the plants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:24.640
are the last specimens of vegetation from a barren&nbsp;
Earth, a final record of green. In High Life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:30.320
the plants are the sole source of food for the&nbsp;
main character and his infant child, a matter of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:37.480
survival. Yet none of these characters is going&nbsp;
to survive for very long — though they continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:43.720
to obsess over their plants’ health, maintaining&nbsp;
their routines even knowing the end is coming. ‘I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:50.360
check on the plants in the greenhouse. I check&nbsp;
for bugs. I check the moisture of the soil. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:58.480
check the dust on the leaves. And when no one is&nbsp;
around, I hum to them.’ Like the crew in Solaris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:04.320
the astronaut in A Star Called Sun is trapped&nbsp;
in their memories, the ship’s interior sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:10.320
distorting into a street corner from their past.&nbsp;
Memories invade the film High Life with similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:16.320
abruptness — vignettes from the main character’s&nbsp;
childhood coming and going without context. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:22.120
seems reflective of how the protagonist’s child&nbsp;
only knows Earth from fragmented images on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:29.120
ship’s corrupted hard drive. Instead of a sun,&nbsp;
the ship in High Life headed towards a black hole.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:36.240
And yet the main character continues to raise his&nbsp;
child, to give her whatever life he can, no matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:42.760
how short. Despite the looming cosmic destruction,&nbsp;
the film argues these moments matter, these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:49.200
memories hold weight. ‘I wish I had made more of&nbsp;
an effort to remember,’ the protagonist says at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:58.600
the end of A Star Called Sun. ‘I wish I had taken&nbsp;
more photos.’ In addition to images, encoded on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:06.080
the Golden Record is the Javanese romantic melody&nbsp;
"Puspawarna" — translating to "kinds of flowers."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:11.760
I think there’s something inspiring about this&nbsp;
song traveling on, in defiance of the cosmos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:17.840
billions of kilometers from the nearest flower.&nbsp;
There’s also something profoundly unsettling about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:25.720
it. Because on an interstellar scale, Voyager&nbsp;
1 has gotten nowhere. Even the closest star is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:32.240
trillions of miles further away. Space is so&nbsp;
much emptier than we realize. When we think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:38.120
of the planets, our mind goes to the model from&nbsp;
classrooms that has them all in a neat row. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:44.680
space doesn’t look like this. It doesn’t even look&nbsp;
like this — that’s still way too close together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:51.480
It looks like this. That tiny, pale blue dot&nbsp;
beneath Saturn’s rings? That’s the Earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:57.640
And everything in space is constantly moving,&nbsp;
hurtling through the dark at breakneck speeds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.640 --> 00:11:02.880
We like to think of that darkness as something&nbsp;
loud, something that ships can audibly woosh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:10.000
through. But without air molecules to vibrate, the&nbsp;
vacuum of space is silent. There are no explosions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:16.760
either — at least not the fiery type from sci-fi&nbsp;
movies. A real space battle would be dead quiet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:23.240
punctuated only with the occasional burst of&nbsp;
heat that would quickly implode. How is anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:31.920
supposed to navigate this vast, silent, alien sea?&nbsp;
In our ongoing search for extraterrestrial life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:37.600
we’ve turned to radio waves to make noise in the&nbsp;
cosmos. Some of these signals have traveled as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:44.520
far out as three hundred trillion kilometers.&nbsp;
Want to see that on a map? Here it is! Sorry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:51.120
I’ll zoom in. Maybe once more. There, that’s&nbsp;
the area our messages have reached. It’s another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:57.160
pale blue dot. And it’s probably an exaggeration,&nbsp;
because most of our signals, which are primarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.160 --> 00:12:03.720
a jumble of old tv and radio dramas, likely fall&nbsp;
apart after that many light years, the memories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:11.360
blurring into meaningless static. In the game The&nbsp;
Anglerfish Project, it is your job to sort through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:18.240
this static. You play as an old man working for&nbsp;
a mysterious company in the far reaches of space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:23.520
using a probe attached to an actual fishing&nbsp;
pole to catch and document wayward signals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:31.280
from Earth. Most are a jumble of nonsense — old&nbsp;
advertisements and PSAs. But sometimes you’ll reel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:38.360
in a love letter, or a fragment of history. It’s&nbsp;
a lonely, isolating experience that encourages you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:44.320
to reflect on the types of messages we’ve blasted&nbsp;
into the void. It made me wonder what an alien&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:51.720
intelligence would even make of our detritus.&nbsp;
Beyond being time capsules, the Golden Records&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:57.840
are also designed to be messages informing&nbsp;
aliens of our planet. After all, the voyager&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:04.040
probes won’t dissipate like radio waves — though&nbsp;
they’re much slower. If by some miracle an alien&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:10.320
found and decoded the record, they’d discover&nbsp;
along with images and music, sounds of the Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:19.160
including chimpanzee noises (screeches). I’m not…&nbsp;
entirely sure an alien would understand the intent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:26.160
behind that one. I’m not entirely sure I do. But&nbsp;
what if, right? What if these records did make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:31.880
contact, what if extraterrestrials understood&nbsp;
their contents, and traced the craft back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.880 --> 00:13:41.640
Earth? The scariest moment of the Outer Wilds&nbsp;
probably wasn’t that time I fell into the sun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:49.560
but when I descended into a fog in the center&nbsp;
of a planet and saw… are those stars? They were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:58.760
in fact, the lights of giant alien anglerfish.&nbsp;
And unfortunately, they noticed me. …Maybe we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.760 --> 00:14:06.080
don’t want aliens to hear us. The ‘Dark Forest&nbsp;
Hypothesis’ is the theory that the reason space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.080 --> 00:14:12.360
seems so devoid of life is because any species&nbsp;
that makes too much noise is extinguished by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:19.320
hostile extraterrestrial life. Science fiction has&nbsp;
long told stories of humans alerting something to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:25.520
our presence we should have left alone. In some&nbsp;
horror fiction, these aliens roll up to our home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:31.680
planet, bringing the destruction directly to&nbsp;
us. But I think the scarier incarnation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:39.040
horror-alien is one encountered in the far reaches&nbsp;
of space. The aliens in these stories often feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:46.000
like personifications of the unknown itself, a&nbsp;
punishment for our blind, hubristic push into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:52.120
the beyond. It’s not just the aliens that make&nbsp;
the edge-of-space-horror genre frightening… It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.120 --> 00:14:58.960
the isolation. Okay, having said that, we now&nbsp;
obviously have to talk about the game Alien:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:05.680
Isolation. Based on the iconic film franchise,&nbsp;
this game has you explore an empty ship — empty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:11.960
except for, of course, the alien. There’s a&nbsp;
litany of techniques the game uses to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:17.920
this creature scary. There’s the fact your weapons&nbsp;
barely work, the fact it can move through vents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:23.040
the fact that your only way of tracking it is&nbsp;
with this tiny analogue screen that forces you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:28.320
glance away from what’s in front of you. But the&nbsp;
most effective technique of all has nothing to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:36.440
with the creature, and everything to do with the&nbsp;
setting. An effective horror-genre space station…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.440 --> 00:15:43.240
is basically a haunted house. Though lacking in&nbsp;
cobwebs and creaking floors, it is a place of dim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.240 --> 00:15:50.600
corridors concealing monsters, one that sequesters&nbsp;
its characters from any hope of rescue. The cosmos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:56.720
beyond the station can be seen as part of the&nbsp;
structure, with space itself a single enormous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.720 --> 00:16:04.080
horror-set-piece — a setting so frightening, you&nbsp;
almost don’t need the alien. Is a space filled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:10.920
with hostile life scarier than a space that’s&nbsp;
empty? Arthur C. Clarke, author of 2001: A Space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:17.960
Odyssey, famously stated: "Two possibilities&nbsp;
exist: either we are alone in the universe or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.960 --> 00:16:26.480
we are not. Both are equally terrifying." Here on&nbsp;
Earth, we are perhaps the most sheltered children&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.480 --> 00:16:32.800
of the universe. Solar wind from our sun shields&nbsp;
us from interstellar radiation, creating an island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:39.040
of safety in the roiling cosmic ocean. Jupiter&nbsp;
defends Earth from asteroids like a protective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:45.120
older sibling with its gravitational pull. Earth’s&nbsp;
magnetic field extends tens of thousands of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:50.960
kilometers into space, safeguarding the surface&nbsp;
from deadly charged particles. Mars and Venus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.960 --> 00:16:58.600
didn’t get magnetospheres at all. It’s difficult&nbsp;
to convey just how inhospitable the other planets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.600 --> 00:17:04.880
in our solar system are. Jupiter’s Great Red Spot,&nbsp;
that little dot on the planet’s surface — is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.880 --> 00:17:11.000
storm larger than the Earth itself. If you were&nbsp;
to descend into this maelstrom, temperatures would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:17.760
rise to over one thousand three hundred degrees&nbsp;
Celsius — around two thousand four hundred degrees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:23.240
Fahrenheit. Not even the cracked, yellowing&nbsp;
surface of Venus, otherwise the hottest planet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.240 --> 00:17:28.640
in our solar system, gets that scorching.&nbsp;
On the opposite end of the spectrum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:35.440
the outer Ice Giants, Uranus and Neptune, spin so&nbsp;
far from the warmth of the sun, their atmospheres&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:43.280
regularly fall hundreds of degrees below freezing.&nbsp;
Beyond our solar system, there are likely billions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:49.520
of exoplanets in our galaxy alone — although we’ve&nbsp;
only managed to spot around five-thousand. Given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.520 --> 00:17:55.040
the sheer number of potential Earth-like worlds,&nbsp;
it seems that there has to be some other form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.040 --> 00:18:01.560
of life out there. But maybe that’s a privileged&nbsp;
Earthling perspective. It seems like other beings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:08.440
must exist because, well, we exist. But until&nbsp;
we know more about the origin of life, it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.440 --> 00:18:16.200
challenging to calculate just how lucky we are to&nbsp;
be here. Because we’ve had it so relatively easy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:21.240
space hasn’t always been a source of fear.&nbsp;
In the past, some cultures assumed it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.240 --> 00:18:27.240
a flat backdrop, a shallow dome of tiny lights&nbsp;
surrounding a globe that sits at the center of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.240 --> 00:18:33.360
the universe. Even as our understanding grew, and&nbsp;
the scale of the solar system became more and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:39.560
apparent, many assumed we’d find other habitable&nbsp;
worlds right next door. In the late 19th and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.560 --> 00:18:46.320
early 20th century, the idea that Mars or the Moon&nbsp;
might be full of undiscovered life didn’t seem so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:52.080
far-fetched. When your only frame of reference&nbsp;
is the lushness of Earth, space seems like it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.080 --> 00:18:59.200
would be jam-packed with extraterrestrials. Though&nbsp;
once a distant dream, space travel, too, was once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:05.360
portrayed more flippantly. In Jules Verne’s 19th&nbsp;
century novel from Earth to the Moon, the lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.360 --> 00:19:12.160
characters make it to the moon by shooting their&nbsp;
vessel out of a giant cannon, because… why not?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.160 --> 00:19:17.960
It’s difficult to imagine the true nature&nbsp;
of space. A place where, without gravity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.960 --> 00:19:24.400
your bones rapidly lose density. A place where the&nbsp;
lack of days and nights create insomnia. A place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.400 --> 00:19:33.080
of silent, inhospitable lifelessness. Perhaps the&nbsp;
closest thing to true “Space Madness” manifested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.080 --> 00:19:41.840
during the 1976 Soviet mission Soyuz 21. The&nbsp;
operation was supposed to be routine — involving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:48.600
a sixty day stay onboard the Salyut 5 space&nbsp;
station. But just a few weeks in, the crew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.600 --> 00:19:55.400
claimed a maddening odor had filled the cabin, a&nbsp;
smell so toxic and persistent the operation had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.400 --> 00:20:02.360
to be called off. Yet allegedly, upon the reserve&nbsp;
crew’s arrival, no unusual smell or fume leakage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.360 --> 00:20:09.200
was detected. The scent was likely an intense&nbsp;
delusion, a nightmare born from claustrophobic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.200 --> 00:20:15.000
conditions mixed with the psychological terror&nbsp;
of the cosmic vastness. …Perhaps, it’s better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:22.200
to remain on the ground. But even in Earth’s&nbsp;
protective embrace, we are not completely safe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.200 --> 00:20:28.640
from the cosmos. Asteroids hurled from the beyond&nbsp;
aren’t just the stuff of disaster movies. Space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.640 --> 00:20:34.160
rocks large enough to trigger mass extinctions&nbsp;
have stuck our planet in the past, wiping out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.160 --> 00:20:40.160
far greater dynasties of life than humanity. Even&nbsp;
with Jupiter looking out for us, the Earth has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:47.680
close calls all the time. And even if we aren’t on&nbsp;
a collision course with an apocalyptic asteroid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.680 --> 00:20:54.080
we are on a collision course with the sun. A few&nbsp;
billion years from now, our star will become a red&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.080 --> 00:21:00.680
giant, and in all likelihood swallow the Earth.&nbsp;
And even if we don’t end in fire, the sun, like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.680 --> 00:21:09.720
all stars in the universe, is eventually going&nbsp;
to die. In 1816, many people became terrified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:09.720 --> 00:21:16.240
that the sun’s death was imminent. Telescopes&nbsp;
discovered massive sunspots growing like mold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.240 --> 00:21:23.120
upon the solar surface, and temperatures began to&nbsp;
drop. This event, called The Year Without Summer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.120 --> 00:21:29.560
saw then-unprecedented snowfall in the Northern&nbsp;
Hemisphere. In the resulting chaos, the somewhat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:29.560 --> 00:21:37.480
infamous Lord Byron was inspired to craft an&nbsp;
apocalyptic eulogy simply titled ‘Darkness.’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:44.320
“I had a dream, which was not all a dream. / The&nbsp;
bright sun was extinguish’d, and the stars / Did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.320 --> 00:21:51.440
wander darkling in the eternal space, / Rayless,&nbsp;
and pathless, and the icy earth / Swung blind and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.440 --> 00:21:59.360
blackening in the moonless air.” Of course, the&nbsp;
sun did not go out in 1816. The sunspots actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:59.360 --> 00:22:04.800
had nothing to do with the falling temperatures&nbsp;
—the winter’s true origin was the eruption of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.800 --> 00:22:11.360
Mount Tambora stratovolcano in the Pacific, which&nbsp;
blanketed the globe in volcanic gas. Though this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:11.360 --> 00:22:17.480
shroud eventually lifted, I see it as a reminder&nbsp;
of how fragile Earth’s atmosphere is, of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.480 --> 00:22:24.880
little change it would take to make our planet&nbsp;
just another barren rock. Perhaps impermanence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.880 --> 00:22:31.680
is the true most frightening aspect of the cosmos.&nbsp;
Even our galaxy won’t endure forever. In roughly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.680 --> 00:22:37.600
four-point-five billion years, the Milky Way will&nbsp;
collide with the Andromeda galaxy, the galactic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.600 --> 00:22:42.160
fender-bender flinging untold stars out into the&nbsp;
void. It makes you wonder what the point is. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.160 --> 00:22:50.040
nothing lasts, if everything in the universe&nbsp;
is on an unchangeable arc towards annihilation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:57.600
why try to preserve anything at all? What meaning&nbsp;
could a single image, a memory, a life — possibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:57.600 --> 00:23:05.920
have? Maybe permeance isn’t what gives something&nbsp;
value. Yes, every plant is going to wilt, every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.920 --> 00:23:12.280
radio signal is going to decay, every marshmallow&nbsp;
is going to burn or be digested. On a cosmic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.280 --> 00:23:19.000
scale, the Golden Record probably isn’t going&nbsp;
to get very far. So what? On a personal level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.000 --> 00:23:24.760
things that have been lost still matter, moments&nbsp;
still linger, the night sky can still move us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:24.760 --> 00:23:33.080
even with the knowledge that one day every light&nbsp;
will go out. ‘The trajectory of this ship is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:33.080 --> 00:23:41.200
unchanging.’ I’m still going to water the plants.&nbsp;
And hey, as always, thank you for watching. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.200 --> 00:23:46.520
enjoyed this video and want to support the&nbsp;
channel, consider liking, subscribing, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.520 --> 00:23:57.880
hitting the notification icon to stay up to date&nbsp;
on all things curious. See you in the next video.

